      "doctor_question": "Did you experience similar difficulty sitting still or staying seated when you were a child?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, absolutely. Even as a girl, my report cards always said the same thing—that I was bright but could not stay in my seat or wait my turn. My mother used to joke that I wore a path between my desk and the pencil sharpener before I was ten. Speaking of pencils, do you remember those old crank sharpeners they used to have bolted to the wall in classrooms? The ones that smelled like cedar shavings? We had one in my fourth-grade class, Mrs. Gable's room. She was a lovely woman, wore these bright floral dresses every day, but she was so strict about margins on our lined paper. I still think about her when I'm grading my own students' essays. They don't even use pencils anymore, it's all typed on tablets now. The school gave them all these cheap little tablets last year, and the screens crack if you just look at them wrong. I must have had to send three kids to the IT office just this week because their screens were shattered. \n\nWait, what were we talking about? Oh, right, being a child. Yes, always up and about, never could just sit still at my desk.",
